{"authored" [{"question" "q-337721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-2(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}).\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t)=(\\frac{2t-2\\ln|t|+C}{(t-1)^2})f(t)+g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{2}{t(t-1)}\n$$" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-337721/review.json" "file_sha256" "72bf7ea819a336326fbfa353f5ac7178656113f472533750a5bd477d80179fcb" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-337721/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "6602492fdd67552345f5e90f8d3b1d89e1d4d30ba1fc773d072f315095f2ef4c" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-337721/verification.json" "basis" 1925 "rationale" "Differentiate the original equation before integrating. The source hint and its matching parametric expression solve a different linear ODE; preserve them as original evidence and correct only the study/solver premise. The direct coefficient answer remains the exact original option c."} {"question" "q-337721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Put $t=y'(x)$. The original equation becomes $y=xt^2-2t$, so $f(t)=t^2$ and $g(t)=-2t$.\n\nOn an interval where $t$ varies, differentiate with respect to $t$ and use $dy/dt=t\\,dx/dt$:\n\n$$t x'=t^2x'+2tx-2.$$\n\nThus $t(1-t)x'=2tx-2$, and for $t\\ne0,1$,\n\n$$x'+\\frac{2}{t-1}x=\\frac{2}{t(t-1)}.$$\n\nThe integrating factor is $(t-1)^2$, giving\n\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}\\big((t-1)^2x\\big)=2-\\frac{2}{t}.$$\n\nIntegration gives\n\n$$x(t)=\\frac{2t-2\\ln|t|+C}{(t-1)^2},\\qquad y(t)=t^2x(t)-2t.$$\n\nThese formulas satisfy both the original equation and $dy/dt=t\\,dx/dt$. Consequently,\n\n$$f(t)+g(t)=t^2-2t.$$\n\nThe parametric branch is interpreted locally where $dx/dt\\ne0$. Constant-slope solutions lost when dividing are $y=0$ ($t=0$) and $y=x-2$ ($t=1$). The original supplied hint and parametric coefficient are erroneous; neither was used in this derivation." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-337721/review.json" "file_sha256" "72bf7ea819a336326fbfa353f5ac7178656113f472533750a5bd477d80179fcb" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-337721/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "6602492fdd67552345f5e90f8d3b1d89e1d4d30ba1fc773d072f315095f2ef4c" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-337721/verification.json" "basis" 1925 "rationale" "Differentiate the original equation before integrating. The source hint and its matching parametric expression solve a different linear ODE; preserve them as original evidence and correct only the study/solver premise. The direct coefficient answer remains the exact original option c."} {"question" "q-337721" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{t}^{2}-2t"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-337721/review.json" "file_sha256" "72bf7ea819a336326fbfa353f5ac7178656113f472533750a5bd477d80179fcb" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-337721/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "6602492fdd67552345f5e90f8d3b1d89e1d4d30ba1fc773d072f315095f2ef4c" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-337721/verification.json" "basis" 1925 "rationale" "Differentiate the original equation before integrating. The source hint and its matching parametric expression solve a different linear ODE; preserve them as original evidence and correct only the study/solver premise. The direct coefficient answer remains the exact original option c."} {"question" "q-337721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-2(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}).\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t)=(\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-6t+{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}})f(t)+g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{2-t}{t(1-t)}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d947b6a7986483625c220c9c4d851f71a997d1dc174c4d51bc49d82bdaf8e7" "basis" 1924 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-337721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{2-t}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2/(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t-1|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =(t-1)^{2}\\frac{2-t}{t(1-t)} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =\\frac{(1-t)(2-t)}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2}) & =\\frac{2-t}{t}(1-t) \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\frac{2-t}{t}(1-t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\int (\\frac{2}{t}-3+t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-3t+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ x & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-6t+{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-6t+{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}-2t,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =(\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-6t+{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}}){t}^{2}-2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(t)={t}^{2},\\text{ }g(t)=-2t,$ and\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)={t}^{2}-2t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/state.json" "file_sha256" "81d947b6a7986483625c220c9c4d851f71a997d1dc174c4d51bc49d82bdaf8e7" "basis" 1924 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-337721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-cap